      "role": "user",
      "content": "the response cut off at the end. can u finish the workout structure part? also, can u tell me a bit about how i should handle the feeling of fatigue or when i feel like i'm hitting a wall during the sessions? like if i'm feeling really unmotivated or just drained mid-workout, what's the best way to push through that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "...weight feels challenging by the last rep, but still allows you to maintain perfect form. If you feel completely gassed or mentally drained mid-workout, it's crucial to listen to your body; you don't want to injure yourself trying to push through a wall that is actually a sign of overtraining. If you feel that deep, grinding fatigue, it's better to stop the set or take an extra long rest period rather than risking a bad lift. Sometimes, the best way to push through is to take a 2-3 minute break, grab some water, and remind yourself why you started this journey.\n\nFor the rest of the week, focus on accessory movements (like lunges, rows, or overhead extensions) for 3 sets of 8-12 reps to build muscle endurance. Keep the rest periods between heavy sets relatively long—2 to 3 minutes—to ensure you can maintain good intensity for the next set.\n\n**Handling Fatigue and Motivation:**\n\nWhen you feel that wall coming, it usually means your central nervous system is tired, not just your muscles. If you are unmotivated, try to shift your focus from the *weight* to the *process*—focus on perfect bracing",
      "source": "target",
